# Untitled

MOFs-derived plum-blossom-like junction \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) as an efficient nitrogen fixation photocatalyst: Insight into the active site of the \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) around oxygen vacancy
State Key Laboratory of High-efficiency Utilization of Coal and Green Chemical Engineering, School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Ningxia University, Yinchuan, Ningxia 750021, China
G R A P H I C A L A B S T R A C T
The three phase composite \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) obtained by calcination of MIL-68-In has high photocatalytic NRR performance and the In of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) near the oxygen vacancy acts as the active site of the catalytic reaction.
## A R T I C L E I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Article history:  Received 1 December 2022  Revised 17 January 2023  Accepted 23 January 2023  Available online 27 January 2023
Keywords:  Ammonia synthesis  Photocatalysis  \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\)  Oxygen vacancies  Active sites
Nitrogen activation with low-cost, visible-light-driven photocatalysts continues to be a major challenge. Since the discovery of biological nitrogen fixation, multi-component systems have achieved higher efficiency due to the synergistic effects, thus one of the challenges has been distinguishing the active sites in multi-component catalysts. In this study, we report the photocatalysts of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) with plum-blossom-like junction structure obtained by one-step roasting of MIL-68-In. The "branch" is carbon for supporting and protecting the structure, and the "blossom" is \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) for the activation and reduction of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\), which form an efficient photocatalyst for nitrogen fixation reaction with the performance of \(51.83\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\). Experimental studies and DFT calculations revealed the active site of the catalyst for nitrogen fixation reaction is the \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) around the oxygen vacancy in \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\). More importantly, the elemental In forms the Schottky barrier with \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) in the catalyst, which can generate a built-in electric field to form charge transfer channels during the photocatalytic activity, not only broadens the light absorption range of the material, but also exhibits excellent metal conductivity.
