## 1. Introduction
With the burgeoning world population, the human demand for ammonia is increasing [1–3]. However, the widely used conventional Haber–Bosh process for the synthesis of ammonia requires high temperature and pressure conditions \((300{-}500\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) \(20-\) \(30\;\mathrm{MPa})\) [4–6]. According to statistics, the ammonia industry consumes \(1\mathrm{-}3\%\) of the world's electrical energy every year and emits large amounts of greenhouse gases [6,7]. Therefore, it is important to explore ammonia production technologies with low energy consumption and less pollution [8]. Photocatalytic nitrogen fixation is a method for synthesizing ammonia at room temperature and pressure with solar energy as driving force, water as the proton source, and nitrogen in air as the reactant [9–11]. 45 years ago, G. N. Schrauzer first found \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -based photocatalyst for nitrogen fixation under ultraviolet light [12]. Recent decades, many different semiconductor materials have been developed for photocatalytic nitrogen fixation reactions [13,14], including \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}\) [15,16], \(\mathsf{W O}_{3}\) [17,18], BiOX(X = Br, Cl, I) [19,20], CdS [21,22], \(\mathsf{M o O}_{3}\) [23,24], MOFs [25,26] and MOFs derivatives [27] etc. Among them, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), composed of metal ions and organic linked ligands, are excellent templates and precursors for the preparation of MOFs derivatives due to their large surface area, ordered structure, and adjustable pores [28]. And the MOFs-derived materials inherit the advantages of MOFs including the uniformly doped heteroatoms, large surface area and controllable structures. Moreover, the derivatives calcined at specific atmosphere and temperature tend to be carbon coated materials with excellent conductivity and pore structure. These advantages often make many MOFs derivatives into excellent semiconductors and play an important role in photocatalysis [29].
Indium \((\mathrm{In})\) oxide, an \(n\) -type semiconductor, with excellent electrical conductivity, strong oxidation properties and good thermodynamic stability, is considered as a promising candidate for photocatalytic nitrogen fixation [30]. However, pure \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) has low catalytic activity due to its low utilization of photon energy in the solar spectrum, poor surface active sites, and fast charge recombination [31]. To circumvent and solve these problems, many new attempts have been made to modify indium oxide, the most classical of which is compounded with other semiconductors or elementary substance. For example, Wang et al. synthesized a novel \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}/\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}\) microsphere heterostructure with oxygen vacancies [31], which migrated electrons from \(\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}\) to \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) under irradiation, and the oxygen vacancy is the catalytic reaction active site with a photocatalytic nitrogen fixation performance of \(40.04\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) . Trong-On Do et al. successfully prepared hollow peanut-shaped \(\mathrm{Ru-In}_{2}0_{3}\) heterojunction and the ammonia production rate was up to \(44.5\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) . The distribution of Ru elements in \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) improves the visible light absorption and the oxygen vacancies in the \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) act as active sites, promoting the catalytic properties of the material [13]. Although many studies have confirmed the oxygen vacancies of metallic oxide is the active sites for NRR [32], few studies have elucidated the effect of other atoms around oxygen vacancies on photocatalytic performance. In addition, the electron transfer between heterojunctions or composites is not clear enough. To better understand the electron transfer pathways between heterojunctions and the effects of other atoms around oxygen vacancies on photocatalytic performance, relevant experimental studies are necessary.
Based on the above analysis, we report a plum-blossom-like junction material \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@C\) which was obtained by one-step calcination of MIL-68-In. The experimental results show that by controlling the calcination conditions, MIL-68-In can be converted into \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@C\) junction catalyst with coexistence of In and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) , which has a specific morphology and the protection of carbon
layer. Notably, the local surface plasmon resonance (LSPR) of elemental In in the catalyst significantly broadens the light absorption range of the material and improved the conductivity of the material. More importantly, elemental In forms a Schottky barrier with indium oxide, which can generate a built-in electric field to form charge transfer channels, and In singlet injects more electrons into \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) . The DFT results indicated that the active site of the nitrogen fixation reaction on \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@C\) is not an oxygen vacancy, but \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) in \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) around the oxygen vacancy. The photocatalyst with good structural stability and cycle life had the nitrogen fixation performance of \(51.83\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , which is higher than the value of most reported NRR photocatalysts.
## 2. Results and discussion
MIL-68-In was synthesized by a simple oil bath method with high yields. The XRD spectra of the obtained MIL-68-In were compared to the theoretical XRD and the results revealed consistent peak positions [33] (Figure S1a), indicating the successful synthesis of MIL-68-In with good crystallinity. Figure S1b showed the SEM image of MIL-68-In was the typical rod shape [34]. Three derivatives were obtained by calcining MIL-68-In in a tube furnace at \(500\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , which was determined by the thermogravimetric curve (TG) of MIL-68-In (Figure S2), under different tube diameters and atmospheres (Scheme 1).
X-ray diffraction (XRD) was used to analyze the composition of the calcined samples. As shown in Fig. 1, the XRD patterns of three derivatives were matched with the cubic \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) phase (JCPDS no. 06-0416). The diffraction peaks at \(30.58^{\circ}\) , \(35.46^{\circ}\) , \(51.03^{\circ}\) , and \(60.68^{\circ}\) were indexed to the \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) lattice planes (22.2), (400), (440), and (62.2), corresponding to the previously reported results [35]. Moreover, additional XRD diffraction peaks appeared in the derivative calcined in lower tube diameter and Ar atmosphere and the new diffraction angles were \(32.96^{\circ}\) , \(36.32^{\circ}\) and \(39.17^{\circ}\) , which belonged to the In phase (JCPDS no. 05-0642) [36], corresponding to (101), (002) and (110) lattice planes of Indium metal, respectively. These characteristics suggested that the two phases of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) and In coexisted and the \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) junction might occur. The colors of the derivatives obtained by calcination in different atmospheres were completely different. \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) obtained by calcination in air is yellow powder, while \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@C\) and \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@C\) obtained by calcination in argon gas atmosphere are black powder, as shown in Figure S3. We preliminarily guess this difference is caused by the presence or absence of carbon.
To further explore the formation process of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@C\) structure during calcination, we calcined the MIL-68-In at different calcination times in the same calcination environment. The XRD spectra of the samples showed the composition of the material phases in Figure S4a. When MIL-68-In was calcined for \(0.5~\mathrm{h}\) , the XRD diffraction peak of the obtained derivative revealed the \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) phase. When the calcination time was increased to \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) , the spectrum showed both elemental indium phase and indium oxide phase, indicating that elemental indium began to be reduced and formed during \(0.5{-}1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) . As the calcination time was extended to \(2\,\,\mathrm{h}\) , the diffraction peak of the XRD pattern did not change, and the two phases still coexist, indicating that the reduction finished and the material tended to be stable. Meanwhile, Raman characterization of the above derivatives with different calcination times were performed to identify the carbon components. As shown in Figure S4b, the characteristic peaks of all materials at \(1340~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1583~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) were corresponding to the D bands and G bands of carbon, which represent the disorderly carbon and graphitic carbon in the material, respectively. The intensity ratio \((I_{D}/I_{G})\) of D and G bands is commonly used to evaluate the material's graphitic degree. The \(I_{D}/I_{G}\) of the derivatives obtained for \(0.5\,\mathrm{~h~}\) , \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) , and
2 h calcination times were 0.90, 0.78, and 0.81, respectively, illustrating that the carbon in MIL-68-In also underwent complex changes during calcination: at \(0{-}0.5\ \mathrm{h}\), MIL-68-In started to pyrolyze and the carbon in the organic ligands formed graphitic and amorphous carbon, while \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) was formed; at \(0.5\ \mathrm{h}{-}1\ \mathrm{h}\), the part of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) undergoes reduction reaction and starts to form mono-
meric In, carbon graphitization is accelerated; finally \(1\ \mathrm{h}{-}2\ \mathrm{h}\), with the end of the reduction reaction, the material structure becomes stable and carbon graphitization decreases.
The Raman characterization of the derivatives obtained from different calcination environments were also performed, as shown in Fig. 1b. \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) exhibited the typical carbon
characteristics at \(1340\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1583\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and the characteristic peaks of carbon did not appear in the spectra of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\). Furthermore, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is conducted to investigate the chemical states of the samples. As shown in Figure S5a: stronger peaks of elemental C were detected in \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\), but the weak carbon signal in \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) might have come from the contaminated carbon in the test [37]. Figure S5b shown the corrected fine spectrum of elemental C. These characteristics confirmed our previous suspicions that under the protection of Ar, the organic ligands in the MIL-68-In precursor were carbonated to carbon-supported indium elemental/indium oxides as a black powder. In contrast, the organic ligands decomposed completely in Ar to form pure \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), which was pale yellow in colour [37]. It is well known that black materials absorb more light [38] and that the presence of carbon would support and protect the structure of catalyst [39], which may have a significant impact on its photocatalytic properties.
The surface composition of the catalysts was further analyzed by XPS fine spectroscopy. The two peaks at \(444.10\;\mathrm{eV}\) and \(451.66\;\mathrm{eV}\) in the In 3d XPS spectrum of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) (Figure S6a) can be assigned to In \(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}\) and In \(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}\), respectively, with a spin-orbit splitting of \(\Delta\mathrm{E_{p}}\) \((3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}\,-\,3\mathrm{d}_{5/2})\,=\,7.6\ \mathrm{eV}\) [36]. The O 1s spectra of the \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) surface were exhibited in Fig. 1d and Figure S6b, and the characteristic binding energy values of \(530.50\;\mathrm{eV}\), \(531.96\;\mathrm{eV}\) and \(533.27\;\mathrm{eV}\) were assigned to the lattice oxygen, oxygen vacancies and adsorbed oxygen, respectively [36]. Compared to \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), the peaks of In 3d and O 1s in both \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) were positively shifted, which could be attributed to electron transfer from \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) to the carbon layer and the electron density dispersion of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) [37]. In comparison to \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\), the \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) peak of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) was positively shifted by \(0.25\;\mathrm{eV}\), and an obvious \(\mathrm{In}^{0}\) peak can be deconvoluted at \(444.57\;\mathrm{eV}\) (Fig. 1c), which indicated that \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) was partially reduced to elemental In and the junction between In and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) [36,37,40]. The oxygen vacancies area of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) was significantly lower than that of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\), indicating that \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) has more oxygen vacancies on the material surface than \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\). The \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) had lower lattice oxygen contents in O 1s spectra, indicating the partial reduction of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) [36].
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) were used to examine the materials surface morphology and crystal structure. SEM, TEM and HRTEM images of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) were shown in Fig. 2a-f. \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) without carbon had lost the rod-like morphology of the precursor, whereas \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) maintained a rod-like morphology, which demonstrated the supporting and protective role of carbon. TEM images showed \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) was composed of many rod-like particles, and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) had the morphology of uniformly loaded particles on a rod-like backbone. The high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image of both \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) demonstrated a lattice spacing of \(0.292\;\mathrm{nm}\), attributed to the (222) crystal plane of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) [41]. Meanwhile, as shown in Fig. 2g, the morphology of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) was plum-blossom-like, with a branch part and a blossom part. The "branch" part was a rod-like structure that kept the shape of the precursor MIL-68-In, and the TEM results showed that the "blossom" part is composed of aggregated particles on the amorphous "branch" part (Fig. 2h). In addition, the HRTEM image of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) showed two lattice spacings (Fig. 2i) of \(0.292\;\mathrm{nm}\) and \(0.247\;\mathrm{nm}\) for \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) (222) and In monomer (002) [36,41], respectively, which were consistent with the previous XRD and XPS results and demonstrated the successful synthesis of junction. Fig. 2j-m showed the energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) spectra of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\). It can be clearly seen that the branch part of the material is mainly disordered C, while the blossom part is mainly In and O elements.
The photocatalytic experiments were carried out under the illumination with a 300 W Xenon arc lamp with air as the nitrogen
curve, the Tauc plot curve of the materials were fitted (Fig. 4b). The band gap of MIL-68-In was \(3.97\,\mathrm{\,eV}\) , and after calcination treatment, the band gaps of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) , \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) were \(2.92\ \mathrm{eV}\) , \(1.77~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(1.47~\mathrm{eV}\) , respectively. A narrower band gap is known to promote the effective use of light and facilitate the generation of more photogenerated electrons [26], which could explain the improved photocatalytic performance of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) .
BET tests on the materials by the adsorption and desorption curves of nitrogen were shown in Fig. 4c. The precursor MIL-68- In showed a typical type II adsorption isotherm with a high specific surface area of \(689.0022\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},\) , which was comparable to the value reported in the literature [44]. Figure S10 shows that its pore size is primarily distributed at \(1.37\;\mathrm{nm}\) , and the pore size near \(56.47\;\mathrm{nm}\) is likely due to the presence of voids between the particles, which will occur similarly to the adsorption of large pores [26]. And after calcination, the BET of the \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) decreased to \(50.1022\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},\) because \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) had lost the rod-like structure of the precursor, which is consistent with the previous TEM results. The specific surface area of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) were \(206.7970\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) and \(226.6157\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},\) , respectively, and the higher BET specific surface
areas than \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) due to the preservation of the rod-like morphology of carbon. Moreover, the specific surface area of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) was relatively superior because the particles of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) are solid cubes, but \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) is blossom shape. The nitrogen adsorption and desorption curves of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) were typical type IV adsorption isotherms, and the hysteresis loop between relative pressures of 0.40 and 1.0 and the pore sizes of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) were mainly distributed at \(1.13\;\mathrm{nm}\) , \(9.87\;\mathrm{nm}\) and \(13.00\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure S10), indicating a micro/mesoporous-like structure [45]. To further confirm the effect of oxygen vacancies for the performance of the samples, electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) was used to detect the number of oxygen vacancies in the bulk phase of the material (Fig. 4d). At \(\mathbf{g}=2.003\) , \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) did not show a signal, indicating that it does not have oxygen vacancies. Both \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) produced strong signals with nearly identical intensities, indicating that both samples contained oxygen vacancies and had similar concentrations. It was noteworthy that the EPR signal of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) after \(6\,\mathrm{{h}}\) of light exposure is greatly enhanced compared to that of fresh catalyst \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) . This indicated that the oxygen vacancies in the bulk phase of the catalyst increase after light exposure,
which is consistent with the improved nitrogen fixation performance in the long time performance test in Fig. 3b. These results all indicated that oxygen vacancies have a significant effect on the catalytic performance of the materials.
The electrochemical active area and electron transfer rate measured by electrochemical methods are also important factors influencing catalytic performance. Shown in Fig. 5a and the table in the inset, MIL-68-In, \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) both had a larger ECSA under light irradiation than under dark, and \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) had larger ECSA than the other materials. It should be noted that the stable result could not be obtained for \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) when Xenon lamps shone on the surface during the ECSA test (Figure S11), indicating the instability of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) without the protective effect of the carbon layer. On the other hand, Fig. 5b showed the impedance magnitude of the materials in light and darkness. Similarly to ECSA, the arc radius of all materials shrank in light, but \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) became smaller than the other materials in both light and darkness, indicating that \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) not only had a larger electrochemically active area but also a lower resistance to transport electron, which accounted for its superior photocatalytic performance.
To further explore the separation and migration efficiency of the photo-generated electron-hole pairs, Linear Scanning Voltammetry(LSV) tests were performed under dark and light conditions, respectively, to determine the bias voltage (0.65 V) used during the
To further explore the separation and migration efficiency of the photo-generated electron-hole pairs, Linear Scanning Voltammetry(LSV) tests were performed under dark and light conditions, respectively, to determine the bias voltage (0.65 V) used during the photocurrent measurements (Figure S12). Photocurrent tests were also performed under different wavelengths of light to investigate the reason for the different catalytic performance of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{C}\), and the difference in current intensity of materials under Ar and Air atmosphere due to the electrons transfer to nitrogen. As illustrated in Fig. 5c, all materials produced a significant light response, with current intensity increasing when exposed to UV–Visible light. Except for \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\), the other \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{C}\), \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) and MIL-68-In produced a difference in current intensity under different atmospheric conditions, indicating certain nitrogen fixation properties. When the light was switched to visible light irradiation (Fig. 5d), \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) produced a strong light response, but the other materials produced weak light response and there was no difference of current intensity at different atmospheres. Combined with the previous DRS test, it's suggested that only \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) had the electrons generation under visible light because \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) contained elemental In, and the LSPR effect of elemental In could expand the absorption and utilization of visible light, which was one of the important reasons for the best photocatalytic nitrogen fixation performance of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) [43,46].
To further investigate the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) reduction process in the photocatalysts, DFT simulations were performed to verify the role of surface oxygen vacancy (Ov) and In monomers on the photocatalytic nitrogen reduction. Figure S13 showed the three optimized structural
models \((\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{V}}\) and \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v})\) for the dominant crystal planes of indium oxide (222) and elemental indium (101). The total and partial density of states were calculated for \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{V}}\) and \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\), and the results were shown in Figure S14. \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) had typical semiconductive properties, but \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) - Ov had metallic properties with enhanced conductivity. In addition, the ordinate value for Fermi level of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\) was greatly increased, suggesting that the metallic properties and conductivity of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\) was further enhanced, which was also in agreement with the experimental results of EIS.
Adsorption of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) molecules on the catalyst surface is the first and most important step in the entire NRR process [47], so we calculated the best adsorption sites of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) on the surface of the \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) - Ov and \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\), respectively. The four possible adsorption sites in \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\), which were In monomer, \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) (not around the oxygen vacancy), oxygen vacancy and \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) around the oxygen vacancy, were shown in Fig. 6a-d. Contrary to most reports, the lowest adsorption energy and possible adsorption site was not on the oxygen vacancy [48], but on the \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) of indium oxide around the oxygen vacancy. We then calculated the adsorption sites of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\) based on the best \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) around the oxygen vacancy) and second-best (oxygen vacancy) adsorption site results of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\). The results (Fig. 6e-f) showed that the adsorption energy of the two \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption sites on \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\) was positive, indicating that \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption at the sites was endothermic reaction and difficult to occur. The electron density difference analysis of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\) and \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\) was further calculated (Fig. 6h and i).
It's showed that there was an obvious charge transfer between the adsorbed \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) atoms with electron accepting and reverse donating processes, and the back-and-forth transfer processes of electrons can weaken the \(\scriptstyle\mathbf{N}=\mathbf{N}\) [48,49]. Furthermore, it can be found that \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\) had more electron transfer by comparing the bader charge, suggesting that In may inject more electrons into \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\), positively contributing to nitrogen activation. In order to verify the speculation, the electron transfer between In and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\) in \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\) was also analyzed by electron density difference analysis and bader charge quantitative calculation (Fig. 6j). We found that In monomer transferred electrons to \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\) and the bader charge was as high as 0.544e, which undoubtedly provided more electron sources for nitrogen activation and greatly helped nitrogen reduction. At the same time, it also meant that the Schotky junction was formed between In and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{v}\), which could generate a built-in electric field to transfer electron from In to \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) and help to achieve the required photocatalytic activity [50].
