Based on the foregoing, the possible mechanism of photocatalytic nitrogen fixation of \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) was shown in Fig. 8. When exposed to sunlight, \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) absorbs UV light, causing electrons in valence band (VB) to be excited to conduction band (CB). At the same time, In monomers also absorb visible light and generate electrons, which are continuously injected into the CB of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\). Then the accumulated electrons are captured by the \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) around oxygen vacancies, and continuously reduce the adsorbed nitrogen. Meanwhile, holes accumulated in the VB of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) can oxidize \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) to \(\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{H}^{+}\), so that \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) can be reduced by electrons and combined with protons to form \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) on the catalyst surface.
## 3. Conclusions
In summary, MIL-68-In was synthesized by hydrothermal method and calcined to obtain a three-phase composite \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) with the performance of photocatalytic nitrogen fixation up to \(51.83\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\). The \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) retains the rod-like morphology of the precursor and has high specific surface area due to the support and protection of carbon. Because the presence of the element In, the three-phase composite junction material \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) has an LSPR effect, allowing the material to absorb visible light for nitrogen fixation reactions. The In singlet forms the Schotky barrier with \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), generating an electric field that can transfer electrons from In to \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) for NRR. Through experimental exploration and theoretical calculation, we found that the \(\mathrm{In}/\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{C}\) obtained by calcination under argon has oxygen vacancies, but the active site of the material is not the oxygen vacancy, and the In of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) near the oxygen vacancy acts as the active site of the catalytic reaction. This study provides a new idea for the study of photocatalytic nitrogen fixation.
